  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 140 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Tuesday night may be the coldest night so far this \nautumn for many locations, with widespread lows in the 40s.\n\nA dry cold front will cross the region tonight, with high pressure \ncentered over Pennsylvania on Tuesday and over the Delmarva \nPeninsula Tuesday night. Wind out of the northeast will already keep \nTuesday's highs a few degrees cooler than Monday's highs, so \ntemperatures will already be slightly cooler when sunset occurs. \nLittle more than a passing high cloud is expected Tuesday night, and \nthe proximity of the high should allow many locations to go calm, \nalthough some light wind out of the northeast could remain across \nsouthern counties. With ideal radiational cooling conditions, evenly \nblended the deterministic NBM with the 10th percentile of the NBM, \nbringing the lows into the 40s in all locations. Later shifts may \nblend in more of a lower percentile and/or take advantage of \nguidance such as the GFSLAMP in shorter time ranges, and cannot rule \nout a location eventually dropping into the upper 30s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Dry weather will continue into the weekend, with \nSunday and Sunday night the most likely time period for any rain.\n\nThere is high confidence in a dry forecast all the way through \nFriday night, with models just beginning to bring some light rain \ninto southern counties late Saturday afternoon. A disturbance \ncurrently over the southwestern Gulf is likely to move to the north \nthrough the week and bring moisture into the southeastern United \nStates for the weekend, but models are inconsistent whether the best \nforcing for precipitation will go north along the Mississippi River \nor go northeast along the Atlantic coastline. With this being days \naway, the forecast will likely continue to change. For now, the \ncurrent forecast brings a 60% chance of rain across southern \ncounties Sunday and Sunday night with a 40-50% chance elsewhere, and \ndiminishing rain chances on Monday.",
